.TH mhvtl-fabrics "1" "@MONTH@ @YEAR@" "mhvtl @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mhvtl-fabrics \- export mhvtl devices to remote initiators over iSCSI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mhvtl-fabrics
[\fB\-c\fR \fIfabrics.conf\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIdevice.conf\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIseconds\fR]
\fBcheck\fR|\fBapply\fR|\fBremove\fR|\fBstatus\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
With the TCMU backend, \fBmhvtl_tcmu_handler\fR creates a LIO user backstore
for every library and drive (\fBcore/user_0/lib\fIID\fR and
\fBcore/user_0/tape\fIID\fR under \fI/sys/kernel/config/target\fR).
.B mhvtl-fabrics
links those backstores into LIO iSCSI targets, as described in
.BR fabrics.conf (5).
It never creates or removes a backstore.
.PP
Each exported library gets its own target. The changer is LUN 0 and each
drive is the LUN of its \fBSlot\fR in
.BR device.conf (5),
so LUN numbers do not change when daemons start in a different order.
Only the initiators listed in fabrics.conf have an ACL; others can neither log
in nor discover the target.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Read both files and print the targets, LUNs and initiators that \fBapply\fR
would create. Touches nothing.
.TP
.B apply
Create the targets, or bring existing ones in line with the files: LUNs,
ACLs and portals no longer listed are removed, the rest is left alone, so
sessions to unchanged targets survive. Waits until every backstore it maps is
enabled. Targets created by an earlier \fBapply\fR whose record was deleted
are removed. Does nothing when \fBMHVTL_BACKEND\fR is set to anything but
\fBtcmu\fR.
.IP
Each target gets iSCSI parameters suited to tape, which issues one command at
a time: \fBInitialR2T=No\fR, \fBImmediateData=Yes\fR, \fBFirstBurstLength\fR
262144, \fBMaxBurstLength\fR 1048576 and \fBMaxRecvDataSegmentLength\fR 262144.
They take effect when an initiator logs in; initiators that offer less
negotiate down. Each ACL gets a \fBdataout_timeout\fR of 30 seconds instead
of LIO's 3, so a short network stall during a write does not close the
connection and fail the tape job; this one also applies to sessions already
logged in.
.TP
.B remove
Remove every target this tool created. Run before the daemons stop, so
initiators see the target leave instead of failing I/O.
.TP
.B status
Print the state of each configured target as one JSON object: whether it
exists and is enabled, its portals with the MTU of the interface holding the
address, its LUNs and their backstores, and for each initiator whether its ACL
exists, whether CHAP is set, and whether it is logged in. Secrets are never
printed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Fabric configuration, default \fI@CONFIG_PATH@/fabrics.conf\fR.
.TP
.BI \-d " file"
Device configuration, default \fI@CONFIG_PATH@/device.conf\fR.
.TP
.BI \-w " seconds"
How long \fBapply\fR waits for the backstores, 0 to 3600. The default is
3 seconds per LUN, at least 60.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/mhvtl/fabrics.state
The targets created by the last \fBapply\fR, so \fBremove\fR finds them even
after their record was deleted.
.SH NOTES
\fBmhvtl-fabrics.service\fR runs \fBapply\fR after \fBmhvtl.target\fR is up
and \fBremove\fR before it stops. It is also part of
\fBmhvtl-tcmu-handler.service\fR, so restarting the handler removes the
targets first and applies them again afterwards. A host that only exports devices can turn
off the local loopback presentation with \fBMHVTL_TCMU_LOOPBACK=no\fR in
.BR mhvtl.conf (5).
.PP
Do not save these targets with \fBtargetcli saveconfig\fR: \fBtarget.service\fR
would restore them at boot, before the handler has created the backstores.
.PP
Firewalls must allow the portal port, 3260/tcp by default.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fabrics.conf (5),
.BR device.conf (5),
.BR vtltape (1),
.BR vtllibrary (1)
